= Boids — Simple =

'''Boids''' is Craig Reynolds's 1987 computer model for simulating flocking-like movement with many simple virtual agents.

Each boid reacts only to nearby boids.

There is no single controller telling the whole flock exactly where to move.

The group pattern emerges from local steering rules.<ref name="reynolds">[https://doi.org/10.1145/37401.37406 Reynolds, C. W. (1987), “Flocks, Herds, and Schools: A Distributed Behavioral Model.”]</ref>

== The three famous rules ==

Boids is usually explained with three tendencies:

'''Separation''' — move away from neighbors that are too close.

'''Alignment''' — turn toward the average direction of nearby neighbors.

'''Cohesion''' — move toward the local center of nearby neighbors.

These rules work together.

Without separation, boids can collide.

Without cohesion, the group can disperse.

Without alignment, headings can remain disorganized.

== Local neighborhoods ==

A boid normally does not examine the entire flock.

It may respond only to neighbors:

* inside a fixed radius;
* inside a field of view;
* among the nearest few agents.

The neighborhood rule changes the result.

A dense swarm can contain many neighbors inside one radius, while a sparse swarm contains few.

== No central leader is required ==

The basic model does not require a leader.

Each boid applies the same local rules.

Large-scale flock motion emerges from those repeated local interactions.

A leader can be added as an extension, but it is not part of the minimal flocking idea.

== Update order matters ==

A simulation usually proceeds in time steps.

For each step:

# find neighbors;
# calculate steering;
# update velocity;
# update position.

A careful implementation computes all steering from the same previous state and then updates all boids together.

If boid 1 moves before boid 2 is evaluated, the result can depend on loop order.

== Speed and steering limits ==

Most boids simulations limit:

* maximum speed;
* maximum steering force or acceleration.

Without a speed limit, repeated steering can make agents move unrealistically fast.

A steering limit creates smoother turns.

These are simulation choices, not laws of bird biology.

== Boundaries ==

A simulated world needs boundary rules.

Common choices include:

* wrap around to the opposite side;
* bounce from walls;
* steer away from edges;
* use open space.

Boundary choice can change flock shape.

Researchers should report it.

== Obstacles and goals ==

More complex boids can also:

* avoid obstacles;
* seek a target;
* flee from danger;
* follow a path;
* wander.

Reynolds later developed a broader library of steering behaviors around these ideas.

Flocking is only one part of that framework.

== Performance ==

A simple implementation compares every boid with every other boid.

For <math>n</math> boids, this can require roughly <math>O(n^2)</math> neighbor checks per step.

Spatial grids or trees can reduce the number of candidate neighbors and make large simulations faster.

== Measuring the flock ==

A flock should not be judged only by appearance.

Researchers can measure:

* directional alignment;
* average distance between neighbors;
* number of collisions;
* flock diameter;
* number of separate groups.

One common alignment measure is called '''polarization'''.

A flock can be strongly aligned but still split into several separate clusters.

== Noise ==

Random noise can make a flock look more natural and can test robustness.

Small noise may preserve coherent motion.

Large noise can break the flock apart.

Noise level is therefore another model parameter.

== Boids is not the same as real flocking ==

Real birds and fish use real sensory systems and are affected by:

* vision;
* aerodynamics or water flow;
* predators;
* individual goals;
* fatigue;
* learning.

The boids model reproduces selected flock-like patterns.

It does not prove that real animals use exactly three rules.

== Boids and swarm intelligence ==

[[Swarm intelligence]] is much broader.

It includes ant-colony optimization, robot swarms, particle swarm optimization, and other decentralized systems.

Boids is specifically a model of collective movement.

== Boids and emergence ==

Boids is a classic example of [[Emergence]] and [[Self-organization]].

The programmer writes the local rules.

The exact group trajectory is not scripted in advance.

That does not mean the behavior is mysterious or undesigned.

The local rules are the mechanism.

